<section class="updates"><div class="updates-grid wrap"><article class="update-entry"><p class="eyebrow">Recently updated</p><time datetime="2026-09-17">17 September 2026 · Research action</time><h3>Case A catalogue and finding-aid comparison</h3><p>The comparison identified conflicting 1939/1940 transition statements and differences in role chronology. Primary legislation remains unexamined.</p><a class="arrow-link" href="#research">See the research update</a></article><aside class="collaboration-call" id="collaborate"><p class="eyebrow">Collaborate</p><h3>Can you help resolve this?</h3><p>Panamensis welcomes documentary leads, corrections, identifications, oral testimony, collection information and research partnerships—especially evidence that can clarify the open question above.</p><a class="arrow-link" href="#about">Ways to collaborate</a></aside></div></section>

<section class="about-method" id="about"><div class="about-method-grid wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow">About / Method</p><h2>A research infrastructure for dispersed evidence.</h2></div><div><div class="about-method-copy"><p>Panamensis is an independent research programme developed by librarian, archivist and researcher Edgardo Civallero. It studies histories of science, nature, environment and knowledge in Panama through connected people, places, organizations, sources, events and biological entities.</p><p>The public site is generated from a filtered research corpus. Claims retain their sources, qualifications and status; disagreements and missing evidence remain visible instead of being silently resolved.</p></div><nav class="about-links" aria-label="About and method links"><a class="arrow-link" href="#about">About Panamensis</a><a class="arrow-link" href="#about">Research method</a><a class="arrow-link" href="#about">Technical architecture</a></nav></div></div></section>
